// Kit-piece runtime metadata (ENH-001, parsed by `hex_core::piece`): everything the runtime knows
// about a kit piece beyond drawing it. `assets/kit/manifest.json` keeps the draw data; a new prop,
// tree or hero piece needs one entry here (the kit gate fails without it, and an entry with a
// missing field does not parse). Entries follow the manifest order.
//
// Fields of a piece:
// - label: what the piece is to the player, the string walk mode reports when it blocks the
//   walker and photo mode reports for a click on it (`PropLabel::as_str`). `None` for a piece
//   nothing can hit or pick. Every obstacle has one (`every_collider_piece_has_a_label`).
// - rise: how a hero grows when its tile is placed: `Tree` scales up, `Building(floors)` rises a
//   floor at a time, roof included (the single-storey longhouse and the low paddock fence in one
//   step; the fence is not a tree: its origin is the tile centre, so a uniform scale would slide
//   its runs in from the border).
// - walk: `Obstacle` (its collider blocks the walker: a sapling at its stem, a shrub at its dense
//   lower dome, a verge bush at its clipped mass, a field boundary by its per-arm boxes),
//   `WalkThrough` (low flora, reeds, lilies, pebbles and the seabed pieces: no collider worth
//   stopping at), `Ground` (a hero the walker stands on and scatter keeps off: the station
//   platform, and the tunnel portals, whose wall tops the walker crosses from the cover meadow
//   and whose faces are steps it cannot climb into the arch; each stands at the tile origin at a
//   whole hex turn, `ground_props_stand_at_the_origin_on_a_hex_turn`), or `None` for a piece the
//   catalog never places as a static prop (traffic, agents, boats, bores). Every prop piece the
//   catalog places has a class (`every_prop_piece_is_classified`).
// - variants: the scatter variants (PRD §6 Variety) a scatter rule naming this piece draws among,
//   after the piece itself. Picks are seeded, so the order is part of every world: never reorder.
// - variant_weights: pick weights of the piece then its variants in order, or `None` to draw each
//   piece equally (a different draw from weights of 1, so `None` stays `None`).
// - verge_variant_weights: weights that replace variant_weights on a road or rail verge tile,
//   or `None` to keep variant_weights there.
// - hero_variants: the variants a hero slot authored as this piece may draw instead, about one
//   slot in three (`hex_core::variant`). A new variant is one more id at the end of its list.
// - footprint: horizontal reach in metres, `ground` the collider's (what it stands on and what
//   others must not overlap) and `crown` its LOD0 canopy's (0 for low props). Scatter, the grass
//   and the walker's fallback hull read it, and `footprints_match_the_kit` measures every one
//   against its GLB. `None` takes the 1.0 m default disc (`Footprint::DEFAULT`): the fence (a
//   line, not a blob: its collider's 14.3 m reach would clear the whole yard), the field
//   boundaries, the ground props, and the pieces scatter never places.
// - ground_box: a rectangular collider's bounds in the piece frame (x, z), before its yaw
//   (`ground_boxes_match_the_kit`). A piece without one is a disc of its footprint ground.
// - fence: a fence's layout in its own frame (x, z), from its `bake-report.json` `layout`
//   block: the line (runs between consecutive points; the gap between the last and first point
//   is the gate opening) and every post's centre and half width (`fences_match_the_kit`: one
//   collider part per run, one LOD0 post per entry).
// - posts: parts that stand outside the piece's collider, each a square's corners in the piece
//   frame; they block the walker as the piece.
// - declared_parts: the walker collider as convex parts in the piece frame, `(x min, x max,
//   z min, z max, top)`, each standing from the GLB collider's foot to its top, in place of the
//   GLB collider's one hull.
// - showcase: `Placed` when a block of the kit scenario's look-dev layout (`kit_scene::kit_layout`)
//   places the piece, else `NotShowcased(reason)` with where it is reviewed instead
//   (`every_showcase_piece_is_placed_or_listed`). The layout positions stay in kit_scene.rs.
//
// parts: collider parts that are not kit pieces of their own, with their label: the junction's
// point lever, a rail viaduct's abutments and piers, and the single-tile landforms' rock feet.
// not_ground: slots of ground pieces that stand on the ground rather than being it: a crop's
// leaning row cards, the tunnel portals' fern cards over their coping, and a rail viaduct's stone
// and deck track (owner RL-Q3: no walking on the deck; the meadow under its arches is the ground
// and its piers are walker obstacles). Nothing stands on them and the walker walks through, or
// under, them.
// car_ground: ground slots a car's wheels rest on: the road, and a level crossing's deck and
// rails. Kerbs, verges, driveway gravel, hedges and gate bars never lift a car.
// centre_obstacles: parts of centre pieces that block the walker, as (centre piece, part,
// footprint corners in the tile frame). A centre's own collider is its whole bed, so a part that
// stands up from it gets a footprint here: the junction's point lever (`_rail_junction.py`
// `lever_parts`: the base timber 0.7 m in from edge 0, 2.6 m off the main line away from corner 0,
// and the lever leaning 0.45 m toward edge 3). The rails and sleepers are walkable ground. A rail
// viaduct's two abutments and two piers (`rail_viaduct_pier`, which grass keeps off too) are the
// stone's widest extent below head height (1.8 m), its faces at z +-3.0: the battered foot on
// decks 2 and 3 (the bake report's `pierFootprintsRuntimeXZ`), the arch ring at 1.8 m on deck 1,
// whose arches spring at 0.5 m. Its deck is not ground (`not_ground`), so the walker walks the
// meadow under the arches.
// footprint_obstacles: landform tiles' standing rock that blocks the walker, as (tile, part,
// convex parts of its footprint in the tile frame). The grass slopes and scree below the rock are
// walkable ground.
